
# Project 03 - Container Network Segmentation

Campus Event Registration System

## Project Overview

This project explores container network segmentation using Docker Compose, Apache HTTPD and PostgreSQL within a simulated campus event registration environment.

The project focuses on frontend and backend network separation, controlled service communication, database isolation, resource management, container hardening, persistent storage and database disaster recovery.

## Technical Objectives

- Deploy Apache HTTPD and PostgreSQL containers.
- Create custom Docker bridge networks.
- Implement frontend and backend network segmentation.
- Configure controlled container network membership.
- Restrict direct database exposure.
- Verify internal service connectivity.
- Apply least-privilege security controls.
- Maintain persistent database storage.
- Perform database backup and restoration.

## Service Configuration

| Parameter | Web Service | Database Service |
|---|---|---|
| Service | events | database |
| Container | events-web | events-db |
| Image | httpd:2.4 | postgres:16 |
| Published Port | 8120:80 | Disabled |
| CPU Limit | 0.65 | 0.5 |
| Memory Limit | 384 MiB | 512 MiB |
| Restart Policy | unless-stopped | always |

## Network Architecture

| Network | Driver | Connected Services |
|---|---|---|
| events-front | bridge | Web |
| events-back | bridge | Web and Database |

The web container is connected to both frontend and backend networks.

The database is restricted to the backend network without a published host port.

This architecture reduces unnecessary network communication and direct database exposure.

## Docker Compose Configuration

```yaml
services:
  events:
    image: httpd:2.4
    container_name: events-web
    ports:
      - "8120:80"
    cpus: 0.65
    mem_limit: 384m
    restart: unless-stopped
    networks:
      - events-front
      - events-back
    privileged: false
    cap_drop:
      - ALL
    security_opt:
      - no-new-privileges:true

  database:
    image: postgres:16
    container_name: events-db
    environment:
      POSTGRES_DB: eventsdb
      POSTGRES_USER: eventuser
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:?Set password}
    cpus: 0.5
    mem_limit: 512m
    restart: always
    networks:
      - events-back
    volumes:
      - events-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

networks:
  events-front:
    name: events-front
    driver: bridge

  events-back:
    name: events-back
    driver: bridge

volumes:
  events-data:
    name: events-data
```

## Security Controls

| Control | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Custom bridge networks | Establish network segmentation |
| Frontend network | Separate frontend service communication |
| Backend network | Restrict database network membership |
| Database port isolation | Reduce direct database exposure |
| privileged: false | Avoid privileged execution |
| cap_drop: ALL | Remove Linux capabilities |
| no-new-privileges | Prevent additional privilege acquisition |
| Resource limits | Control resource consumption |

## Deployment and Verification

```powershell
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps
docker stats --no-stream
docker network ls
docker network inspect events-front
docker network inspect events-back
docker port events-db
docker logs events-web
docker exec events-db pg_isready -U eventuser -d eventsdb
```

Web service testing is performed using localhost on port 8120.

## Network Verification

```powershell
docker run --rm --network events-back alpine nslookup events-db
docker run --rm --network events-back postgres:16 pg_isready -h events-db -U eventuser -d eventsdb
docker run --rm --network events-front alpine nslookup events-db
```

| Verification Test | Expected Result |
|---|---|
| Web network membership | Frontend and backend |
| Database network membership | Backend only |
| Backend database name resolution | Successful |
| Backend database readiness | Accepting connections |
| Frontend database name resolution | Unsuccessful |
| Published database port | None |

Network verification focuses on confirming container network membership, internal name resolution and database accessibility.

A failed DNS lookup alone does not establish complete network isolation.

## Persistent Storage and Recovery

| Parameter | Configuration |
|---|---|
| Database | eventsdb |
| Test Table | registrations |
| Original Volume | events-data |
| Backup Directory | C:\EventsBackup |
| Backup Archive | events-data.tar.gz |
| Restored Volume | events-restored |

The recovery procedure involves stopping PostgreSQL, creating a physical volume backup, restoring the archive into a separate volume and verifying the original database records.

The final Compose configuration uses events-restored as an external volume after restoration.

